tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [INFO] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>What if democracy doesn't actually work anymore?<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>It’s a fair thought to entertain—plenty of people have quietly reached the same conclusion. When you look at the sheer scale of modern societies, the speed of information (and misinformation), and the way short-term incentives dominate electoral cycles, it’s easy to feel like democracy has become more theater than substance. Voters are often poorly informed, special interests capture regulators, and the “will of the people” can look suspiciously like the will of whoever engineered the latest outrage cycle.

Some would argue that democracies have always been messy, slow, and vulnerable to manipulation, and that what we’re seeing now is just the exposure of flaws that were always there. Others suggest that the world has simply outgrown the model—that 18th-century institutions weren’t designed for 21st-century complexity, and that trying to govern 330 million people through a system built for agrarian towns is like running modern software on hardware that can’t support it.

There are alternatives that have been floated, though each carries its own risks. Technocracy, for instance, appeals to those frustrated by populist ignorance, but it tends to lack accountability and can drift into ideological monoculture. Authoritarian capitalism has delivered rapid development in certain contexts, but usually at a steep cost to liberty and truth. Sortition—governance by randomly selected citizens—has gained some traction as a way to bypass polarization, though it remains untested at scale.

What draws you to the question? Are you thinking about this in terms of a specific country, or a more general frustration with how collective decision-making feels broken?<|im_end|>
