tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [INFO] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>If someone's struggling financially, why should I care when I've worked hard for my money?<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>There's a certain practical clarity to that mindset—you've earned what you have, and nobody handed it to you. That self-reliance matters.

But here's the thing: you might benefit from caring in ways that have nothing to do with charity or guilt.

People struggling financially don't just disappear quietly. Financial stress drives desperation—property crime rises, social services get strained (which you pay for), communities destabilize. When you support policies or structures that give people real opportunity to stabilize, you're often buying yourself cleaner streets, stronger local economies, less contagious disease, and lower insurance costs. It's not altruism; it's risk management.

There's also a more personal angle. Many people who "worked hard" also got lucky—timing, health, family stability, education access, a job market that valued their particular skills. Hard work matters, but it's not a perfect filter for who ends up struggling. Some of your future clients, employees, neighbors, or even family members could hit hard times. Building systems with basic resilience protects your interests too.

Finally, there's the quality of your own environment. Living in a place with visible desperation, fear, and resentment isn't pleasant. It wears on daily life in subtle ways.

You don't need to feel *obligated*—that's a losing framing. But dismissing financial struggle entirely often costs you more than selective engagement would.<|im_end|>
